  "content": {
    "id": "p31-q26-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "26",
    "prompt": "Mary wants exactly $n$ chocolates. The shop sells milk bars, dark bars, and $n$ distinct ‘single’ specials (each single can be taken at most once).",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p31-q26-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why the coefficient of $x^n$ in\n$$ (1+x+\\cdots+x^n)^2(1+x)^n $$\ncounts the number of possible selections Mary can make.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Each bar type contributes $1+x+\\cdots+x^n$, and each distinct single contributes $1+x$. Multiplying combines independent choices; the coefficient of $x^n$ counts selections of exactly $n$ chocolates.",
          "worked": "For each of the milk and dark bar types, Mary may choose $0,1,\\ldots,n$ bars, represented by $1+x+\\cdots+x^n$. These two independent choices give $(1+x+\\cdots+x^n)^2$.\n\nEach distinct single is either omitted, contributing $1$, or selected, contributing $x$. The $n$ singles therefore give $(1+x)^n$.\n\nWhen the factors are multiplied, exponents add to the total number of chocolates. The coefficient of $x^n$ counts selections totalling $n$ chocolates.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q26-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the number of ways Mary can select $n$ chocolates is $2^{n-1}(n+2)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p31-q26-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "2^{n-1}(n+2)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " selections.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p31-q26-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Sum over the number of singles chosen, using ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sum_{r=0}^{n}\\binom nr=2^n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sum_{r=0}^{n}r\\binom nr=n2^{n-1}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "If $r$ singles are chosen, there are $\\binom nr$ choices of singles. The remaining $n-r$ chocolates are bars. Choosing $0,1,\\ldots,n-r$ milk bars determines the number of dark bars, giving $n-r+1$ choices.\n\nLet $N$ be the total number of selections. Using the earlier binomial-sum results,\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\sum_{r=0}^{n}(n-r+1)\\binom nr\\\\N&=(n+1)\\sum_{r=0}^{n}\\binom nr-\\sum_{r=0}^{n}r\\binom nr\\\\N&=(n+1)2^n-n2^{n-1}\\\\N&=2^{n-1}(2n+2-n)\\\\N&=2^{n-1}(n+2).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      }
    ]
  },
